  "teamId": 142,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Minnesota Twins entered the season with measured optimism, and their early returns were a fair reflection of that cautious hope. April was a grind — a 13-15 record bookended by some genuinely painful moments in the bullpen and from opposing hitters who feasted on Minnesota's relievers. The Twins were competitive, but barely, and the margin for error felt razor-thin every night at Target Field.\n\nMay and June offered a small measure of encouragement. Austin Martin emerged as a genuine offensive force, and Brooks Lee continued to grow into the middle-of-the-order presence the organization had envisioned when they drafted him. The 14-13 June record masked some ugly individual moments — Anthony Banda's late-game meltdowns and Joe Ryan's struggles against elite lineups — but the team was treading water, which, given the early turbulence, felt like something worth acknowledging.\n\nJuly was the high point, and it came with real drama. Royce Lewis, finally healthy and dangerous, delivered a walk-off triple of enormous consequence late in the month, and Kody Clemens — a revelation at the power position — crushed a grand slam that registered as the single most impactful positive play of the entire season. The Twins went 15-9 in July, their best month by a considerable margin, and for a few weeks, a late playoff push felt genuinely possible.\n\nAugust erased that dream. A 10-17 collapse, driven by a bullpen that continued to bleed in crucial spots, pushed the Twins back toward .500 and ultimately below it. September has offered flickers — Walker Jenkins announced himself with a home run, Travis Adams picked up a crucial double play, and Royce Lewis has continued to produce — but at 77-85, the arithmetic is unforgiving. The Twins finish as a team that peaked in July and never quite found a way to sustain it.",
